TROUBLESHOOTING
Kali (Alice as user name)

The typical process of doing this would have been as follows:
A.Reset the password via recovery mode (most common)

Since I had physical or console access:
I initially had to reboot the machine
At the GRUB menu, chose Advanced options for Kali
Select Recovery mode
Drop to a root shell
Reset the password using the system’s password management tools
Reboot and log in normally

OR

B.Reinstall Kali (clean but destructive)
If you don’t care about existing data or want the safest route:
Back up files if possible
Reinstall Kali
Set a new password during installation

HOWEVER, WHILE ATTEMPTING STEP A, THERE WAS NO CLEAR PATH TO SELECTING A RECOVERY MODE

FOR STEP A:
Use GRUB to reset the password
Restart the VM

When the Grand Unified Bootloader (GRUB) menu appeared, I selected Advanced options for Kali
In new VM linux, after advanced options, I was given two Linux GNU. 
1.Kali GNU/Linux, with 6.16.8+Kali
2.Kali GNU/Linux, with 6.16.8+Kali AMD64
Chose option 1
But the system went back to LOG-on page

Started the process again but this time Highlighted option 1:
Kali GNU/Linux, with 6.16.8+Kali
Pressed e (this edits the boot parameters)
Scroll down to the line that starts with “Linux” and ends with ‘ro quiet splash” or just “ro quiet” and add “init=/bin/bash”
“linux /boot/vmlinuz-6.16.8+Kali root=UUID=... ro quiet splash init=/bin/bash”
Then boot the system
Press Ctrl + X or F10
The command line reboots and stops at:
bash: no job control in this shell
root@/_

The attached “init=/bin/bash” made the filesystem writable which by default was read-only.
Run:
root@/_mount -o remount,rw /

Optional check
root@/_mount | grep " / "
“/dev/sda1 on / type ext4 (rw,relatime,errors=remount-ro)”
That means your root filesystem is writable
The mount -o remount,rw / command worked successfully
You can now change passwords

Reset the password
If you are a user, with username which is often “ kali”
Type: passwd kali

Or reset root:
Type: passwd root

Set a new password when prompted.

Reboot properly
Run:
exec /sbin/init

If that doesn’t work:
reboot -f

Log back in as either “root” or “user”.
